# TaskTails environment (INF-16). Copy to .env.local and fill in.
# Never commit real secrets — .gitignore covers .env*, this template excepted.
#
# WHICH FILE, WHERE
#   Local dev        .env.local        Next reads it automatically; the Prisma
#                                      CLI is pointed at it by prisma.config.ts.
#   Docker Compose   .env              Compose only reads `.env`, not .env.local.
#   EC2 / Vercel     the environment    Set these on the instance or in the
#                                      project settings. Do not ship a file.
#
# Deployment recipes — AWS, Neon, Vercel — are at the bottom.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Required everywhere
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Signs the JWT session cookie (NFR-GEN-4). Generate one with:
#   npx auth secret
# Use a DIFFERENT value per environment. Rotating it invalidates every session,
# which mid-study means logging every participant out — pick it once and keep it.
AUTH_SECRET=

# See the connection-string recipes at the bottom. The value below is the
# docker-compose Postgres, which is what local development uses:
#   docker compose up -d db && npx prisma migrate dev
#
# ONLY correct in .env.local. If you are filling in a `.env` for Compose, either
# delete this line — the app service already defaults to the right thing — or
# change the host to `db`. `localhost` inside the container means the container
# itself, and the app will fail to reach the database.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://tasktails:tasktails@localhost:5432/tasktails?schema=public"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Google OAuth (AUTH-08, AUTH-09) — optional
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Create credentials at https://console.cloud.google.com. The authorised
# redirect URI is <origin>/api/auth/callback/google — add one per environment:
#   http://localhost:3000/api/auth/callback/google
#   https://<your-domain>/api/auth/callback/google
# Leave both blank and the "Continue with Google" button hides itself; the app
# runs fine on credentials alone (see isGoogleEnabled in src/auth.ts).
AUTH_GOOGLE_ID=
AUTH_GOOGLE_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deployment
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The app's public origin. NextAuth cannot infer it behind a proxy, so OAuth
# callbacks and post-sign-in redirects go to the wrong host without it.
# Vercel sets this itself — leave it unset there.
AUTH_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Tells NextAuth to believe the forwarded host headers. Required behind nginx,
# an ALB, or anything else terminating TLS in front of the app. docker-compose.yml
# already sets it for the container.
# AUTH_TRUST_HOST=true

# Published host port for the container (INF-15). Override when `npm run dev`
# already holds 3000; the container always listens on 3000 internally.
APP_PORT=3000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Connection-string recipes
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Prisma 7 takes the URL through the pg driver adapter (src/lib/prisma.ts), not
# through `datasource db`. That means node-postgres parses it — so `sslmode` and
# friends in the query string are honoured, and they behave the way pg says, not
# the way libpq says.
#
# THE SSL TRAP, and it is a real one:
#   In pg 8.x, `sslmode=require` is treated as an alias for `verify-full` — it
#   verifies the certificate chain AND the hostname, and prints a deprecation
#   warning telling you so. libpq's `require` means "encrypt, don't verify", so
#   a URL copied from AWS or psql docs does something stricter here than you
#   expect. In pg 9 the meaning flips to libpq's. Both facts argue for saying
#   exactly what you mean:
#
#     sslmode=verify-full                    verify chain + hostname (strictest,
#                                            no warning, stable across pg 9)
#     sslmode=verify-full&sslrootcert=<pem>  ...against a specific CA
#     uselibpqcompat=true&sslmode=require    encrypt, do not verify
#     sslmode=disable                        no TLS (local only)
#
#   Verified against this stack: sslmode is parsed and enforced by the adapter.
#
# --- Local (docker-compose) ------------------------------------------------
#   postgresql://tasktails:tasktails@localhost:5432/tasktails?schema=public
#   From inside the compose network the host is `db`, not `localhost`.
#
# --- AWS RDS Postgres (NFR-GEN-3, the Free Tier target) --------------------
#   postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@INSTANCE.REGION.rds.amazonaws.com:5432/tasktails?schema=public&sslmode=verify-full&sslrootcert=/app/certs/rds-global-bundle.pem
#
#   RDS presents a certificate from Amazon's own RDS CA. If verify-full fails
#   with a certificate error, that CA is not in the container's trust store:
#   download the bundle from
#   https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/UsingWithRDS.SSL.html
#   and point sslrootcert at it (COPY it into the image). Only if you cannot,
#   fall back to `uselibpqcompat=true&sslmode=require` — that encrypts the
#   connection but authenticates nothing, so it is a downgrade, not a fix.
#
#   Keep the database in the same VPC and security group as the EC2 instance and
#   do NOT give it a public address; the study holds participant data.
#
# --- Neon (the fallback in NFR-GEN-3, ~$7/month) ---------------------------
#   postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@ep-xxx.REGION.aws.neon.tech/tasktails?schema=public&sslmode=verify-full
#
#   Neon uses a publicly trusted CA, so verify-full works with no extra file.
#   Neon gives two hosts: the pooled one (`-pooler` in the name) and the direct
#   one. Use POOLED for the app, DIRECT for `prisma migrate deploy` — migrations
#   through a transaction pooler fail on statements a pooler will not carry.
#
# --- Vercel -----------------------------------------------------------------
#   Set AUTH_SECRET, DATABASE_URL and the two Google values in Project Settings
#   → Environment Variables, per environment. Leave AUTH_URL unset: Vercel
#   provides the origin.
#
#   Serverless functions open a connection per invocation, so a direct Postgres
#   URL will exhaust the connection limit under any real load. Use a pooled
#   endpoint (Neon's `-pooler` host, or RDS Proxy).
#
#   Note the app is designed for the EC2 path (INF-15, NFR-GEN-3). Vercel is
#   documented as an escape hatch, not the deployment that gets tested.
